# Structured Output

## Overview

Your support desk wants every incoming ticket sorted into a category and a
priority, with a one-line summary, as data your code can match on. This
Livebook defines that shape once as a Zoi schema, asks the model for it, and
reads the reply as typed data. By the end, this works:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:ok, ticket} = Zoi.parse(MyApp.Triage.schema(), reply.data.output)

ticket
# => %{category: :shipping, priority: :normal, summary: "Customer asks where order A-1001 is."}
```

You'll also see what happens when the model returns data that breaks the
schema, and when Jido AI starts a repair round. The model runs offline.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}, {:plug, "~> 1.0"}])
```

`plug` provides `Req.Test`, which the offline model uses.

## The Triage Schema

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.Triage do
  @moduledoc "The ticket triage shape shared by the model request and your code."

  @schema Zoi.object(
            %{
              category: Zoi.enum(billing: "billing", shipping: "shipping", account: "account"),
              priority: Zoi.enum(low: "low", normal: "normal", high: "high"),
              summary: Zoi.string() |> Zoi.min(1)
            },
            coerce: true,
            unrecognized_keys: :error
          )

  def schema, do: @schema
  def json_schema, do: Zoi.to_json_schema(@schema)
end
```

Each option matters at one boundary:

| Option | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `Zoi.enum(shipping: "shipping", ...)` | The model sees the string `"shipping"`; your code gets the atom `:shipping`. |
| `coerce: true` | Accepts the string keys that come back from JSON. |
| `unrecognized_keys: :error` | Emits `additionalProperties: false`, which OpenAI strict schemas require. |

## The Request Schema

The ReAct `output_schema` field takes a JSON Schema map, not a Zoi struct.
Convert once with `Zoi.to_json_schema/1`:

```elixir
MyApp.Triage.json_schema()
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
%{
  type: :object,
  required: [:priority, :category, :summary],
  properties: %{
    priority: %{type: :string, enum: ["low", "normal", "high"]},
    category: %{type: :string, enum: ["billing", "shipping", "account"]},
    summary: %{type: :string, minLength: 1}
  },
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  additionalProperties: false
}
```

The same schema now drives the provider request, ReqLLM's validation of the
reply, and your own parse of the result.

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.TriageAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "triage_agent",
    id: "my_app/triage_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      instructions "Triage the support ticket. Pick one category and one priority, and summarize it in one sentence."
    end
  end
end
```

This Agent has no tools. With an OpenAI model, ReqLLM sends a request that
has both `output_schema` and tool definitions as a forced
`structured_output` tool call, and the ReAct output guard rejects that call
as a tool outside the allowlist. To get structured output from an Agent that
has tools, send `tool_policy: %{requirement: :disabled}` in that request so
no tool definitions are sent.

## Offline Model

This cell is the fake provider. It answers ReqLLM's HTTP requests with
OpenAI Responses API JSON, in the order you queue them, and sends each
request body to this notebook:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OfflineModel do
  @moduledoc "Queues fake OpenAI Responses API replies for ReqLLM."

  @doc "Request options that send model calls to this module."
  def options do
    %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 256,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    }
  end

  @doc "Queues one reply per model call and reports each request body."
  def respond_with(bodies) do
    owner = self()

    Enum.each(bodies, fn body ->
      Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
        {:ok, request, conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
        send(owner, {:model_request, Jason.decode!(request)})
        Req.Test.json(conn, body)
      end)
    end)
  end

  @doc "A final text answer. Structured output arrives as JSON text."
  def text(text) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp_1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg_1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [%{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => []}]
        }
      ],
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => 20,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => 5,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => 25
      }
    }
  end

  @doc "Returns the request bodies received so far."
  def requests(acc \\ []) do
    receive do
      {:model_request, body} -> requests([body | acc])
    after
      0 -> Enum.reverse(acc)
    end
  end
end
```

## Request An Object

Put the JSON Schema in the request's `output_schema` field:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(
    ~s({"category":"shipping","priority":"normal","summary":"Customer asks where order A-1001 is."})
  )
])

agent = MyApp.TriageAgent.instantiate!(id: "triage-1")

signal =
  MyApp.TriageAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Hi, where is my order A-1001? It was due yesterday.",
    output_schema: MyApp.Triage.json_schema(),
    options: MyApp.OfflineModel.options()
  })

{:ok, next_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: reply}]} =
  MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

{reply.data.status, reply.data.output}
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
{:completed,
 %{
   "category" => "shipping",
   "priority" => "normal",
   "summary" => "Customer asks where order A-1001 is."
 }}
```

With `output_schema` set, `reply.data.output` is the decoded object, with
string keys. Without it, `output` is the answer text.

A Zoi struct in the field fails request validation before any model call:

```elixir
bad_signal =
  MyApp.TriageAgent.request_signal!(%{
    input: "Where is my order?",
    output_schema: MyApp.Triage.schema()
  })

{:error, error} = MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, bad_signal)
Exception.message(error)
```

The result is `"invalid type: expected map, at output_schema"`.

## Read Typed Data

Parse the reply with the same Zoi schema:

```elixir
{:ok, ticket} = Zoi.parse(MyApp.Triage.schema(), reply.data.output)

ticket
```

The result is
`%{category: :shipping, priority: :normal, summary: "Customer asks where order A-1001 is."}`.
Match on it like any map:

```elixir
case ticket do
  %{priority: :high} -> :page_on_call
  %{category: :billing} -> :billing_queue
  %{category: :shipping} -> :shipping_queue
  _ticket -> :general_queue
end
```

The result is `:shipping_queue`.

The Session records the object as the assistant message, JSON-encoded, so the
next request in the conversation sees it:

```elixir
{:ok, messages} = Jido.AI.Thread.Projection.messages(next_agent.state.session)

Enum.map(messages, &{&1.role, &1.content})
```

The result is:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
[
  user: "Hi, where is my order A-1001? It was due yesterday.",
  assistant: "{\"category\":\"shipping\",\"priority\":\"normal\",\"summary\":\"Customer asks where order A-1001 is.\"}"
]
```

## What The Model Saw

Jido AI passes the schema to ReqLLM as an object output with strict
validation. For this OpenAI request, ReqLLM sent it as a strict JSON Schema
response format:

```elixir
[request] = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests()

format = request["text"]["format"]
{format["type"], format["name"], format["strict"], format["schema"]["required"]}
```

The result is
`{"json_schema", "output_schema", true, ["priority", "category", "summary"]}`.
Other providers receive the schema in their own structured-output form;
ReqLLM owns that mapping.

## Invalid Output

ReqLLM validates the decoded object against the schema before Jido AI sees
it. Here the model invents a category:

```elixir
MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(
    ~s({"category":"returns","priority":"normal","summary":"Customer wants to return A-1001."})
  )
])

{:ok, failed_agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: failed}]} =
  MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

Map.take(failed.data, [:status, :output, :reason_code, :model_calls, :retries])
```

The result is
`%{status: :failed, output: "", reason_code: :model, model_calls: 1, retries: 0}`.

```elixir
String.starts_with?(failed.data.reason, "Structured output failed final validation:")
```

The result is `true`. The rest of `reason` names the failing path,
`#/properties/category`.

A schema violation is a failed model call, not a repair candidate. It isn't
retryable, so ReAct doesn't spend `limits.retries` or try `fallback_models`.
Text with no JSON object fails the same way, with
`"Structured output failed final validation: No complete structured output value was materialized."`.
No assistant message is committed:

```elixir
Jido.AI.Thread.Projection.messages(failed_agent.state.session)
```

The result is `{:ok, []}`. To try again, send a new request.

## Repair

A repair round starts when the output passes the schema but fails the ReAct
output guard. The guard rejects a final answer that is empty, longer than
4,000 characters (an object is measured as encoded JSON), cut off by a
`length`, `content_filter`, or other non-`stop` finish reason, or given
before a required tool round.

Here the first reply is valid but its summary runs to 4,100 characters:

```elixir
too_long =
  Jason.encode!(%{
    category: "shipping",
    priority: "normal",
    summary: String.duplicate("x", 4_100)
  })

MyApp.OfflineModel.respond_with([
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(too_long),
  MyApp.OfflineModel.text(
    ~s({"category":"shipping","priority":"normal","summary":"Customer asks where order A-1001 is."})
  )
])

{:ok, _agent, [%Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply{signal: repaired}]} =
  MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

Map.take(repaired.data, [:status, :model_calls, :retries])
```

The result is `%{status: :completed, model_calls: 2, retries: 1}`.

The second model call carried one extra system message:

```elixir
second = MyApp.OfflineModel.requests() |> List.last()

second["input"]
|> List.last()
|> get_in(["content", Access.at(0), "text"])
```

The result is
`"Repair the previous response. model output is too long. Return a valid final answer."`.

Repair rounds come from the same budget as retries:

| Remaining budget | Next step |
| --- | --- |
| Model attempts ≤ `limits.retries` (default `1`) | Repair with the same model. |
| Another entry in `fallback_models` | Repair with the next model. |
| Neither | `status: :blocked`, `reason_code: :output`, `reason` from the guard. |

`limits.model_calls` and the other budgets still apply; a repair that would
exceed them ends with `reason_code: :limits`.

## A Default Schema

To give every request the same schema, set `output_schema` in the
`reasoning` block. Defaults must be compile-time literals, so write the JSON
Schema map out; a `Zoi.to_json_schema/1` call there fails with
`output_schema requires a compile-time literal value`:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
ai do
  reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
    output_schema %{
      "type" => "object",
      "properties" => %{
        "category" => %{"type" => "string", "enum" => ["billing", "shipping", "account"]},
        "priority" => %{"type" => "string", "enum" => ["low", "normal", "high"]},
        "summary" => %{"type" => "string", "minLength" => 1}
      },
      "required" => ["category", "priority", "summary"],
      "additionalProperties" => false
    }
  end
end
```

Keep the Zoi schema next to it for parsing the reply. A request can still
send its own `output_schema`, or `nil` for plain text.

## Summary

- `output_schema` is a ReAct request field that takes a JSON Schema map.
  Build it with `Zoi.to_json_schema/1`.
- `reply.data.output` is the decoded object with string keys. Parse it with
  the same Zoi schema for atoms and validated types.
- ReqLLM validates the object strictly. A violation ends the request with
  `status: :failed` and `reason_code: :model`, without a repair round.
- The output guard starts a repair round for empty, oversized, or truncated
  answers. Repairs share the `limits.retries` and `fallback_models` budget.
- Request structured output with no tool definitions in the call: an Agent
  without tools, or `tool_policy: %{requirement: :disabled}`.

